You asked an app to turn a folder of photos into a contact sheet. The
window is still open, the progress bar hasn't moved, and the fan is
running. Is the app working, waiting, or no longer doing anything?
Closing the window is one possible response, but it answers none of
those questions. We will look at what the operating system can tell us
before asking anything to stop. Our practice target will be a timer
that does no work and owns no data.

\begin{objectives}
\item Tell the difference between a command, a process, and a process ID.
\item Inspect one process and its parent without disturbing either.
\item Ask a practice process to stop, then check what happened.
\end{objectives}

\section{A process is not a name}
\label{sec:31-identity}
\index{process}
\index{process ID}
When you run \cmd|sleep 60|, the file containing the program is not
itself asleep. Instead, there is a running instance of that program.
That instance is a \FirstUse{process}. A second \cmd|sleep 60|
can run at the same time without becoming part of the first one.
Same instructions, two instances. The operating system assigns each
an identifying number, called a process ID or PID. It is like the
number assigned to a request rather than the name of the service
fulfilling it. The number can be recycled after the request ends.

A command line and a process are not interchangeable, either. The
shell can change directories itself when you type \cmd|cd|; no
external program is needed. Conversely, a line with a pipeline may
start several processes. Even a single application can run separate
processes for its interface, a helper, and the actual conversion work.
A process may contain several threads, which are separate lines of
execution within that process. Here we are identifying the process
from the outside, not counting the activities inside it.

Figure~\ref{fig:31-family} shows a shell starting two copies of one
program. They have distinct PIDs but can have the same parent
process ID, or PPID. A parent need not still be around. When it
exits, the operating system can give its surviving children another
parent. A snapshot of a process's parent is therefore evidence about
the present, not a guaranteed family history.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-31/family.tex}
\caption{One shell can start two instances of the same program. The
numbers illustrate relationships; they are not examples to signal.}
\label{fig:31-family}
\VisualDescription{Shell PID 120 has arrows to timer PIDs 121 and
122. Both timers show parent PID 120.}
\end{figure}

\section{Take a snapshot}
\label{sec:31-snapshot}
\index{ps@\texttt{ps}}
Run the examples in an ordinary interactive \cmd|sh| (type \cmd|sh|
to start one if you use a shell with different variable syntax).
The process you create here is yours and has no files to save. Do
not use printed example PIDs, a PID from another terminal, or a PID
left in a variable from yesterday's practice.

\begin{terminal}
printf 'shell PID: %s\n' "$$"
ps -p "$$" -o pid -o ppid -o comm
\end{terminal}

The shell replaces \cmd|$$| with its PID in this ordinary interactive
shell. The two commands should identify the same number. In
\cmd|ps|, \cmd|-p| selects by PID. Each \cmd|-o| requests a
column: \cmd|pid| is the selected process, \cmd|ppid| is its
parent, and \cmd|comm| identifies the command. Formatting and the
reported command name can differ. Here is an illustrative result:

\begin{terminaloutput}
  PID  PPID COMMAND
 6702  6587 sh
\end{terminaloutput}

The prompt returning is not the shell exiting: the shell is simply
ready for another command. \cmd|ps| has already taken its snapshot
and exited. Try comparing a broader snapshot with that one:

\begin{terminal}
ps -A -o pid -o ppid -o comm | head -n 8
\end{terminal}

The selection \cmd|-A| asks for all processes \cmd|ps| can report in
your environment; containers and local access rules can limit the
view. \cmd|head -n 8| keeps the first eight lines including the
header. This is a display limit, not a filter for importance. You
may see a system process or a service first, and your shell may not
appear at all. A short sample is useful for comparing formats, not
for proving that something is absent.

\begin{quicktable}{M{.94in}YY}
\toprule
Field & Question it answers & What it does not prove\\
\midrule
pid & Which instance did this row describe? &
That the number will still refer to it later.\\
ppid & Who was the parent at this snapshot? &
Which app, window, or login originally started it.\\
comm & What command identity does this view show? &
Which document it was given, or that a similar row is expendable.\\
args & What command and arguments can this view show? &
That the text is complete, current, or safe to share.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

To see a line with arguments, try the next example on the timer you
start. A trailing ampersand asks the shell to start the command and
not wait for it before accepting another line. The shell replaces
\cmd|$!| with the identifier of the most recent asynchronous command
in this shell. Save it immediately; a new background command can
change it. In this simple case it identifies the timer.

\begin{terminal}
sleep 120 &
timer=$!
printf 'my timer PID: %s\n' "$timer"
ps -p "$timer" -o pid -o ppid -o args
\end{terminal}

You should see the timer's command, with its duration where the
arguments are available. If it disappeared during the demonstration,
start a new timer and save its PID again before proceeding; don't
reuse the old number. A background launch can succeed in the shell's
sense even when the command immediately fails to start. Inspecting the
row is worth the extra line. The shell may also print a job number;
that is its own label, not the PID.

\begin{terminal}
ps -p "$timer" -o pid -o ppid -o time -o comm
\end{terminal}

Here \cmd|time| is cumulative CPU time, not the time since the process
started. An idle timer spends almost no time computing while it
waits. The displayed resolution may be coarse. If a converter has
used little CPU, it might be waiting for disk or input; it might also
be finished between snapshots. A burst of CPU use does not prove
that the output is getting better. Before deciding a process is hung,
look for progress in the application, listen for an input request,
and check evidence such as whether a destination file is still
growing. No single field is a verdict.
Figure~\ref{fig:31-snapshots} is the reason to avoid a
strong conclusion from two quiet-looking rows: they leave
nearly the entire interval unobserved.

Suppose the timer row has a PPID that differs from the shell
PID you printed. Check whether you launched the timer from
another tab, put a wrapper around the launch, or copied a stale
number. If you started it in this shell using the lines above,
the shell is normally its parent. But it is easy to run
\cmd|sh script.sh| and forget that a new interpreter is doing
the launching. You can inspect the parent separately using
the same \cmd|ps -p| and \cmd|-o| syntax. This is a diagnostic
comparison, not a permission test and not a request to stop
anything. When helping somebody else, ask them to compare
the numbers locally rather than share the whole running
process list.

The newest visible row is not always the cause of a problem.
An application might start a helper to show a progress window
and run the real export in a second helper. CPU time for the
window may stay unchanged throughout; the worker's CPU time
may increase. If the worker disappears and the window stays,
that tells you only that the worker is no longer in your view.
The job might have completed, failed, or delegated its work.
Check the application's own records before drawing a conclusion.
You can have several useful observations and still not have
safe grounds for acting on a particular PID.

