\section{A request is not a receipt}
\label{sec:31-signals}
\index{signal}
\index{kill@\texttt{kill}}
For a real application, prefer its own Cancel or Quit action. It
knows which subtask to stop and may offer to save, wait for a write
to finish, or keep the previous version. If you need to act from a
shell, a \FirstUse{signal} is a notification sent by the operating
system to a process. The \cmd|kill| command asks the system to send
one. Despite the name, not all signals end a process, and not all
requests to end one are carried out immediately.

\begin{quicktable}{M{.82in}YY}
\toprule
Request & Usual purpose & Important limit\\
\midrule
TERM & Request termination; the default action is to terminate. &
A program can handle it, ignore it, or have it temporarily blocked.\\
INT & Interrupt work, commonly from the terminal interrupt key. &
A program may handle the interrupt differently from TERM.\\
KILL & Force termination when ordinary shutdown has failed. &
The program cannot handle it and cannot run its own cleanup.\\
Signal 0 & Ask whether the target can be signaled without sending a
signal. & It cannot establish that this is the instance you meant.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Here is the request to use on the practice timer. Run this only just
after confirming the row is still that timer in this session. The
example uses the symbolic name instead of a numeric signal code,
and a single positive PID. Do not substitute zero, a negative
number, or a command name: those have other meanings or are not a
PID at all.

\begin{terminal}
kill -s TERM "$timer"
printf 'request status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|-s TERM| specifies the signal. The request status is zero if
the utility reports success; that is not confirmation that the
program shut down gracefully. A nonzero status can follow from
permission restrictions or from a process that exited already; read
the error before doing anything else. Don't add administrative
privileges to overcome the error. If something doesn't match the
practice setup, let the timer finish on its own.

\begin{terminal}
wait "$timer"
printf 'child status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|wait| asks the current shell to collect the status of its own
child. Here it normally reports a nonzero status after termination
by signal. Do not depend on a particular numeric value. If the
timer finished naturally before the request, the child's status can
instead be zero even when the request failed. Some shells print a
separate ``Terminated'' notice. \cmd|wait| is not a general-purpose
tool for processes from another shell; Chapter~\ref{ch:32} develops
that boundary. If you have not sent the signal, waiting here may
take up to the timer's remaining duration.

The separate steps in Figure~\ref{fig:31-request} matter if
you are making notes for someone else. Write down the request
result before the child status, and say how you obtained
each. ``Status 143'' alone doesn't explain which command
reported it. The reusable timer example in the source
listings demonstrates these two observations together; the
individual instructions here let you inspect the timer and
decide whether to proceed before sending the signal.

\begin{terminal}
ps -p "$timer" -o pid -o ppid -o comm
unset timer
\end{terminal}

The old row is usually gone. A header without the row is not a
crash. If a row appears, don't assume the timer resisted: the PID
might have been assigned to something else. Dropping the variable
is a small habit that discourages a future action on an old target.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-31/request.tex}
\caption{Check the outcome separately from the signal request.}
\label{fig:31-request}
\VisualDescription{The shell asks the operating system to send a
signal. It later checks the child status in a separate step.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{Can I just use the strong one?}
\Brian{Why not always send KILL? That sounds definitive.}
\Stephen{It denies the program a chance to shut down cleanly.}
\Brian{But you don't even know that TERM will finish the job.}
\Stephen{Correct. TERM leaves the program an opportunity, not a guarantee.}
\Brian{What if I ask politely and the bad output is already on disk?}
\Stephen{Then termination won't roll it back. Check the output separately.}
\Brian{So the first question is what I can afford to interrupt.}
\Stephen{Yes; often the app's own Cancel is the better first request.}
\DialogueConclusion{Stopping work does not undo it.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{When a real process is in doubt}
\label{sec:31-recovery}
Start by identifying what matters to preserve. If a large export
can be stopped and redone, first verify that the old original is
still intact. If an editor has an unsaved draft, prefer waiting or
the application's recovery tools. If the process belongs to
another account, a service, or a shared machine, stop and ask the
owner; you may not be able to see who depends on it. A job that
appears misbehaved to one person can be doing the work another
person needs.

A forced stop can leave a partial output file or skip saving
settings. It cannot reverse changes already written. After an
interruption, keep originals and any existing backup versions in
place, inspect the destination before opening or replacing it, and
save a new attempt under a different name. Some editors and other
apps offer recovery on restart, but treat recovery as a possibility,
not a plan. If your process is waiting on the storage system, even a
forced stop may not disappear from the process list immediately.

A listed process can also be finished but not yet collected by its
parent. Some \cmd|ps| formats call this a zombie or annotate the
command as defunct. It is not actively finishing your export, and
sending it another signal cannot make its parent collect it. If the
parent is not a practice shell you own, gather evidence rather than
trying to kill the family tree.

On Linux, both \cmd|ps -e| and the procps \cmd|ps ax| style are often
seen. The latter's no-dash syntax belongs to another option style;
it isn't a rule for forming new options. In particular,
\OpenBSD{} \ManPage{ps}{1} uses \cmd|-e| to display the environment,
not to select all processes. An environment can contain confidential
values; don't add that option while looking for a wider list.
Use \cmd|-A| for this example. On macOS and the BSDs, local
\ManPage{ps}{1} pages describe their own columns and defaults.
Consult the local page before relying on process-state letters or
adding Linux-specific columns to a script. Finally, don't share a
full process listing casually: a command line can contain paths,
hostnames, and values supplied by another program, possibly even
credentials. Use fictional data when learning, and see
Chapter~\ref{ch:36} for report hygiene.

\section{Read the evidence in order}
\label{sec:31-triage}
Imagine you launched two exports from different windows. Both
are conversions of the same type, and the process names in a
list are identical. You care about which output each is writing,
not which has the larger number. First check whether the windows
offer a progress or cancellation control of their own. If they do,
you don't need to map the PID to a task from outside: the app
already has that connection. If they don't, the arguments may show
the destination, but a program can take its destination from
an open dialog or a configuration file rather than arguments.
In that case, identical rows and similar family trees might
not provide enough evidence to select a target. A different
view may help you observe; it still does not authorize a stop.

Think also about the order of observations. If you print the
first timer's PID, leave for lunch, and return to an unchanged
terminal display, you haven't confirmed that the timer still
exists. The old screen is a record of an earlier snapshot.
Take a new snapshot immediately before acting. Even with a
fresh snapshot there is a gap: the process can exit and its
PID can eventually be reused. This is why the exercise
targets a recently started, long-running timer you created
in that same shell, and why it tells you not to retry on a
stale number. It is also why sampling a list and blindly
signaling every matching row is a bad repair strategy.

After a stop, check in the same order: what the app reports,
what the known child reported if it was yours to wait for,
and what you can independently verify about its result.
For a contact sheet that might mean opening it in a viewer,
counting the pages you expected, and checking that the original
photos are where you left them. A displayed thumbnail could
come from an older file at the same path; a fresh output
name improves the comparison. Don't take a vanished process
as evidence of either successful or unsuccessful export.

\begin{exercise}{Find your shell, not somebody else's}
\label{ex:31-shell}
Print the shell PID, then ask for just the PID and command name
columns of its row. Check which number matches. Make no changes.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run \cmd|printf '%s\n' "$$"| and
\cmd|ps -p "$$" -o pid -o comm|. The PID in the selected row
matches the first number; the command name need not be identical
to the basename of your account's default shell. There is nothing
to remove or stop.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Watch one timer finish}
\label{ex:31-finish}
Start an eight-second timer in the background, record its PID, and
ask for both PID and PPID. Then collect its status without
interrupting it. If it finishes before you inspect it, try again.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
sleep 8 &
short_timer=$!
ps -p "$short_timer" -o pid -o ppid
wait "$short_timer"
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
unset short_timer
\end{terminal}
If you saw the row, its PID was the saved number. In the absence
of interruption the timer exits successfully, so the status should
be zero. Waiting collects the timer and the last line clears the
variable.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Distinguish the two statuses}
\label{ex:31-term}
Repeat the timer practice with a new \cmd|sleep 120|. First
identify the exact row, then send TERM and print both the request
status and the status obtained by waiting. What does each measure?
Clear the PID variable after collecting the child.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run the \cmd|sleep 120 &| and \cmd|timer=$!| lines, select the
row with \cmd+ps -p "$timer" -o pid -o args+, and verify that
it is the new timer. Run \cmd+kill -s TERM "$timer"+, print
\cmd|"$?"| immediately with \cmd|printf|, then run
\cmd|wait "$timer"| and again print \cmd|"$?"| immediately.
The request may succeed (zero) while the child's status is nonzero
because it ended by signal. End with \cmd|unset timer|. If the
row had already vanished, skip the signal and just collect the
child. Leave the practice \cmd|sh| with \cmd|exit| if you started one.
\end{solution}

\begin{takeaways}
\item A command name belongs to a program or invocation; a PID selects
an instance for a limited time.
\item A process list is a snapshot. Confirm the target and prefer the
app's own shutdown control before sending a signal.
\item A signal request, the child's exit status, and file integrity are
three different outcomes.
\end{takeaways}

